From f3f9cbbc7788d1519318dc8c4c753c3e73b36f56 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 10:33:35 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_ui: the agent panel's cards fade in; the latency report
 measures frames
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Zeo next §6.52, first step (ask 76; operator, 2026-11-17: start with the
cheapest and measure it). A card that appears in the agent panel now fades
in over 150 ms with an ease-out instead of popping in: the tasks card in
each of its three states (expanded, the pill, the bar docked at the
bottom), the /btw answer card, the question card and its minimized bar.
With reduced motion (the `reduce_motion` setting, which Zed already
honours for the cursor and the scrollbar) a card appears at once.

How: agent_ui::card_fade gives a card's root a `.fade_in(id, cx)` in place
of its final `.into_any_element()`, so the animation sits on the card's own
root and no wrapper changes its layout -- the tasks card is placed by its
overlay from the root's size, and a wrapping div would have stretched the
pill to the free area. GPUI keeps an animation's start in the element
state of its id and drops that state when the element is not drawn in a
frame, so a card fades again each time it reappears and not on the frames
in between. Only appearing is animated: fading out needs the card kept on
screen after its state says it is gone, which none of these cards supports
yet. The floating thread controls are left alone: they are always present,
and their hover change is a style, not an appearance.

Measured, not assumed: `dev: dump input latency histogram` now ends with
the window's frame durations -- how long a frame took to draw (p50, p90,
p99, max) and, when GPUI recorded any, the intervals between frames
presented while animating -- from gpui's own frame profiler
(Window::frame_duration_snapshot). On Xvfb (llvmpipe, a debug build), the
same steps -- a prompt that brings the tasks card up, then five round
trips between the expanded card and the bottom bar, ten fades -- were run
with `reduce_motion` on and off (hub docs/assets/zeo-6.52-card-fades-
2026-11-26/):

                    reduce_motion on    fades
  frames drawn            166            262
  draw p50             19.22 ms       19.28 ms
  draw p90             31.21 ms       24.92 ms
  draw p99             50.43 ms       42.37 ms
  draw max             64.19 ms       63.41 ms

A fade adds its frames (about ten each) and costs nothing per frame. GPUI
recorded no frame as "presented while animating" on Xvfb in either run, so
the interval between animated frames is not measured at this rung, and a
debug build on llvmpipe draws above a 60 Hz budget with or without fades;
a release build on a GPU is the operator's live check.

Tests: card_fade's end state (fully opaque at the end, invisible at the
start, clamped against an easing's overshoot) and its duration bound; the
frame section of the report (percentiles named, the slowest frame kept,
"No frames recorded yet." without frames). The agent_ui suites that paint
these cards (tasks card, /btw, question card) pass with the fades on.
---

